\subtree{\title{Context (not from the slide)}
\taxon{remark}
\p{Marked as context, not poster claims: RNEL's bidirectional line implants microelectrode arrays in motor and \strong{somatosensory} cortex of people with spinal cord injury. ICMS of area 1/S1 evokes graded, spatially-specific tactile sensations on the paralyzed hand, closing a sensorimotor loop for prosthesis control. The distinct-qualia claim — that separable electrodes map to separable, nameable percepts — is exactly the "distinct qualia" axis this category is about; the engineering claim here is that the percept-to-electrode map stays usable over a decade of implantation.}
}

\p{Why it matters for us: the invasive endpoint of the "distinct qualia" axis — the same axis our noninvasive multi-sensory stimulation direction ([[bcf-0056]]) approaches from the other side. Maps to \strong{[I] High-Bandwidth Invasive} (Blackrock/Utah array) in [[bcf-0051]].}
